          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Why does acid reflux tend to happen after meals?",
          "answer": "Acid reflux is linked to meals mainly because of how the stomach and its valve behave. The passages I found explain part of this, but not all of it.\n\n- **The valve relaxes.** Reflux usually happens through transient relaxation of the lower esophageal sphincter, the ring of muscle between the esophagus and stomach. When it relaxes at the wrong time, stomach contents can move upward (GERD summary, condition-gerd).\n- **Fat slows emptying.** Fatty meals slow how quickly the stomach empties. This extends the time reflux can occur, which is why a heavy late dinner can provoke symptoms more than the same food at midday (Eating with reflux, lifestyle-gerd-diet).\n- **Gas and alcohol add to it.** Carbonated drinks add gas that stretches the stomach directly. Alcohol relaxes the valve and increases acid exposure (lifestyle-gerd-diet).\n\nThe retrieved material did not explain the effects of meal size or body posture (such as lying down after eating), though these are often discussed in this context. I can't confirm them from the sources I searched, so I won't describe them further.\n\nGuidance also suggests that people with reflux track which foods seem to bring on symptoms, rather than avoiding a long list of common triggers. Weight reduction, where body weight is above the healthy range, has among the strongest supporting evidence (lifestyle-gerd-diet). A clinician can help if symptoms keep happening or do not respond to changes.\n\nSeek emergency care straight away for chest pain or pressure, especially with shortness of breath, sweating, or pain spreading to the arm, neck or jaw. These can resemble heartburn, and they need urgent assessment.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
